description.sr:
Napiši funkciju `groupBy(items, keyFn)` koja grupiše elemente niza po ključu.

`items` je niz, a `keyFn` je funkcija koja za jedan element vraća njegov ključ. Funkcija vraća objekat u kome je svaki ključ povezan sa nizom elemenata koji su dobili taj ključ.

Pravila:
- elementi unutar grupe zadržavaju redosled iz ulaznog niza
- prazan niz daje prazan objekat `{}`
- ključevi objekta su uvek tekst, pa ključ koji je broj postaje tekst: `1` i `'1'` završavaju u istoj grupi
- ključevi koji liče na ugrađena imena objekta (`constructor`, `toString`) rade kao svaki drugi ključ
- ulazni niz se ne menja

Primeri:
```
groupBy(['one', 'two', 'three', 'four'], (w) => w.length);
// { 3: ['one', 'two'], 4: ['four'], 5: ['three'] }

groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], (n) => n % 2);
// { 0: [2, 4], 1: [1, 3, 5] }

groupBy([], (x) => x);        // {}
groupBy([1, '1'], (x) => x);  // { 1: [1, '1'] }
groupBy(['constructor'], (x) => x);  // { constructor: ['constructor'] }
```

description.en:
Write a function `groupBy(items, keyFn)` that groups the elements of an array by a key.

`items` is an array and `keyFn` is a function that returns the key of one element. The function returns an object in which every key is linked to an array of the elements that got that key.

Rules:
- elements inside a group keep the order of the input array
- an empty array gives an empty object `{}`
- object keys are always text, so a numeric key becomes text: `1` and `'1'` end up in the same group
- keys that look like built-in object names (`constructor`, `toString`) work like any other key
- the input array is not changed

Examples:
```
groupBy(['one', 'two', 'three', 'four'], (w) => w.length);
// { 3: ['one', 'two'], 4: ['four'], 5: ['three'] }

groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], (n) => n % 2);
// { 0: [2, 4], 1: [1, 3, 5] }

groupBy([], (x) => x);        // {}
groupBy([1, '1'], (x) => x);  // { 1: [1, '1'] }
groupBy(['constructor'], (x) => x);  // { constructor: ['constructor'] }
```

tests:
test({ sr: 'grupiše reči po dužini', en: 'groups words by length' }, () => {
  expect(groupBy(['one', 'two', 'three', 'four'], (w) => w.length)).toEqual({ 3: ['one', 'two'], 4: ['four'], 5: ['three'] });
});
test({ sr: 'ključ može biti ostatak pri deljenju', en: 'the key can be a remainder' }, () => {
  expect(groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], (n) => n % 2)).toEqual({ 0: [2, 4], 1: [1, 3, 5] });
});
test({ sr: 'prazan niz daje prazan objekat', en: 'an empty array gives an empty object' }, () => {
  expect(groupBy([], (x) => x)).toEqual({});
});

hiddenTests:
test('elements keep their order inside a group', () => {
  const people = [
    { name: 'Ana', city: 'NS' },
    { name: 'Bojan', city: 'BG' },
    { name: 'Cana', city: 'NS' },
    { name: 'Dejan', city: 'NS' },
  ];
  const g = groupBy(people, (p) => p.city);
  expect(g.NS.map((p) => p.name)).toEqual(['Ana', 'Cana', 'Dejan']);
  expect(g.BG.map((p) => p.name)).toEqual(['Bojan']);
});
test('numeric and text keys end up in the same group', () => {
  expect(groupBy([1, '1', 2], (x) => x)).toEqual({ 1: [1, '1'], 2: [2] });
});
test('the input array is not changed', () => {
  const items = [3, 1, 2];
  groupBy(items, (n) => n % 2);
  expect(items).toEqual([3, 1, 2]);
});
test('the same key for all gives one group', () => {
  expect(groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c'], () => 'all')).toEqual({ all: ['a', 'b', 'c'] });
});
test('the groups contain the same objects, not copies', () => {
  const a = { id: 1 };
  const g = groupBy([a], (x) => x.id);
  expect(g[1][0]).toBe(a);
});
test('keys named like built-in object properties work', () => {
  expect(groupBy(['constructor', 'toString', 'a', 'constructor'], (x) => x)).toEqual({ constructor: ['constructor', 'constructor'], toString: ['toString'], a: ['a'] });
});
test('a single element gives a single group', () => {
  expect(groupBy(['x'], (s) => s.toUpperCase())).toEqual({ X: ['x'] });
});

solutions.code:
function groupBy(items, keyFn) {
  const groups = {};
  for (const item of items) {
    const key = keyFn(item);
    if (!Object.hasOwn(groups, key)) groups[key] = [];
    groups[key].push(item);
  }
  return groups;
}

text.sr:
Ako grupa za ovaj ključ još ne postoji, pravimo prazan niz. Zatim dodajemo element u grupu.

- `Object.hasOwn(groups, key)` pita da li objekat ima **sopstveno** svojstvo sa tim imenom. Ne koristimo `!groups[key]`: prazan objekat nasleđuje svojstva kao `constructor` i `toString`, pa bi za ključ `'constructor'` ta provera našla ugrađenu funkciju umesto praznog mesta i `push` bi pukao.
- `groups[key]` radi sa uglastim zagradama jer je ključ promenljiva, i pretvara ga u tekst, zato `1` i `'1'` dele grupu.

```
groupBy([1, 2, 3], (n) => n % 2):
item=1: key=1, groups nema svoje svojstvo 1 → [], dodaje 1   → { 1: [1] }
item=2: key=0, groups nema svoje svojstvo 0 → [], dodaje 2   → { 1: [1], 0: [2] }
item=3: key=1, groups ima svoje svojstvo 1  → dodaje 3       → { 1: [1, 3], 0: [2] }
```

text.en:
If the group for this key does not exist yet, we create an empty array. Then we add the element to the group.

- `Object.hasOwn(groups, key)` asks whether the object has its **own** property with that name. We do not use `!groups[key]`: an empty object inherits properties such as `constructor` and `toString`, so for the key `'constructor'` that check would find the built-in function instead of an empty place and `push` would fail.
- `groups[key]` uses square brackets because the key is a variable, and it turns it into text, which is why `1` and `'1'` share a group.

```
groupBy([1, 2, 3], (n) => n % 2):
item=1: key=1, groups has no own property 1 → [], adds 1   → { 1: [1] }
item=2: key=0, groups has no own property 0 → [], adds 2   → { 1: [1], 0: [2] }
item=3: key=1, groups has own property 1    → adds 3       → { 1: [1, 3], 0: [2] }
```

solutions.code:
function groupBy(items, keyFn) {
  return items.reduce((groups, item) => {
    const key = keyFn(item);
    const group = Object.hasOwn(groups, key) ? groups[key] : [];
    groups[key] = [...group, item];
    return groups;
  }, {});
}
